# Terraform working files. All of it is regenerated by `terraform init`, and a
# stale copy is a confusing way to make a plan disagree with the repository -
# .terraform/ caches the provider binaries and the module source, so a moved
# tag or an edited module hides there until something surprising happens.
.terraform/
tfplan
*.tfplan
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Terraform state. This repo uses a remote azurerm backend, so a local state
# file means something ran outside CI. Committing one would publish the full
# contents of the infrastructure, including everything the state considers
# sensitive.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

###############################################################################
# Deliberately NOT ignored - read this before copying in a standard template
###############################################################################
#
# terraform.tfvars.json
#   This file IS the repository. It is the deployment data the agent writes and
#   CI validates against schemas/app-stack.schema.json. The stock GitHub
#   Terraform .gitignore excludes *.tfvars and *.tfvars.json; adopting it here
#   would silently stop every deployment from being tracked, and the failure
#   would look like "the agent's PR is empty" rather than like a .gitignore
#   problem.
#
# .terraform.lock.hcl
#   Should be committed - it is what pins provider versions to exact builds.
#   None exists in this repo yet, so each CI run resolves azurerm afresh within
#   `~> 4.20` and two runs can legitimately use different patch versions.
#
###############################################################################

# Local tooling. The validator's dependencies belong in a venv, not in the
# repository - see scripts/validate_deployment.py.
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Editors and OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
